---
import Component from "../../layouts/Component.astro"
import Anatomy from "../../components/Anatomy.astro"
import AutoExample from "../../components/AutoExample.astro"
import Conditional from "../../components/Conditional.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import PropOrClass from "../../components/PropOrClass.astro"
import { BarChart as UIBarChart } from "@opui/astro"

import BarChartCSS from "@opui/css/components/bar-chart.css?raw"

import barChartApi from "../../component-api/bar-chart/api"

export const description =
  "A data table drawn as a column chart with CSS, with an axis, gridlines, grouped series and tooltips."
---

<Component
  description={description}
  slug="bar-chart"
  heroAnatomy
  apis={[{ title: "Bar chart API", api: barChartApi }]}
  browserSupport={[
    "alt-text-generated-content",
    "attr",
    "container-queries",
    "exp-functions",
    "has",
    "light-dark",
    "relative-color",
    "round-mod-rem",
    "starting-style",
  ]}
  changelogPaths={[
    {
      path: "packages/opui/components/BarChart/BarChart.astro",
      type: "component",
    },
    { path: "src/docs/components/bar-chart.astro", type: "docs" },
    { path: "src/component-examples/bar-chart", type: "docs" },
    { path: "packages/opui/css/components/bar-chart.css", type: "css" },
    { path: "src/component-api/bar-chart", type: "api" },
  ]}
  installationTabs={[
    { title: "bar-chart.css", code: BarChartCSS, lang: "css" },
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="title">Bar chart</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    <p>
      A <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> drawn as a column chart. The chart is the data
      table, so screen readers, print, copy and paste and forced colors get the real
      numbers, with no JavaScript.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use it for dashboard-sized bar and column data: a few dozen bars in up to
      three series. For line, area, pie or scatter charts, thousands of points,
      zooming or locale-formatted axes, use a chart library. See also: <DocLink
        href="/components/table"
        class="ui-link">Table</DocLink
      >, <DocLink href="/components/progress" class="ui-link">Progress</DocLink
      >.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="anatomy">
    <Anatomy>
      <UIBarChart
        caption="Orders"
        label="Month"
        max={560}
        rows={[
          { label: "Jan", values: [412, 448] },
          { label: "Feb", values: [455, 501] },
          { label: "Mar", values: [498, 560] },
        ]}
        series={["2025", "2026"]}
        size="small"
        style="inline-size: 18rem"
      />
    </Anatomy>
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="basics">Basics</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="astro"
        >Pass the categories in <code>rows</code>, each with a <code>label</code
        > and its values, and name the series in <code>series</code>. <code
          >label</code
        > names the category column for screen readers. The axis runs from zero to
        a round number above the largest value.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >Each <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> is a category, with a row header and one <code
          >&lt;td&gt;</code
        > per series. Give every cell its number in <code>--value</code>, and
        the table the largest value in <code>--max</code>. The axis runs from
        zero to a round number above it.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="svelte"
        >Pass the categories in <code>rows</code>, each with a <code>label</code
        > and its values, and name the series in <code>series</code>. <code
          >label</code
        > names the category column for screen readers. The axis runs from zero to
        a round number above the largest value.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="vue"
        >Pass the categories in <code>rows</code>, each with a <code>label</code
        > and its values, and name the series in <code>series</code>. <code
          >label</code
        > names the category column for screen readers. The axis runs from zero to
        a round number above the largest value.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>

    <AutoExample name="Basics" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="series">Series</h2>
    <p>
      With two or three series, the bars group by category, the series headers
      become the legend, and the values show on hover and focus.
    </p>

    <AutoExample name="Series" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="colors">Colors</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="astro"
        >Set <code>color</code> on a row to <code>"critical"</code>, <code
          >"info"</code
        >, <code>"neutral"</code>, <code>"success"</code> or <code
          >"warning"</code
        > to color its bars. Use it with a single series, and say what the color means
        in the caption or the text.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >Add <code>.ui-critical</code>, <code>.ui-info</code>, <code
          >.ui-neutral</code
        >, <code>.ui-success</code> or <code>.ui-warning</code> to a <code
          >&lt;tr&gt;</code
        > to color its bars. Use it with a single series, and say what the color means
        in the caption or the text.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="svelte"
        >Set <code>color</code> on a row to <code>"critical"</code>, <code
          >"info"</code
        >, <code>"neutral"</code>, <code>"success"</code> or <code
          >"warning"</code
        > to color its bars. Use it with a single series, and say what the color means
        in the caption or the text.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="vue"
        >Set <code>color</code> on a row to <code>"critical"</code>, <code
          >"info"</code
        >, <code>"neutral"</code>, <code>"success"</code> or <code
          >"warning"</code
        > to color its bars. Use it with a single series, and say what the color means
        in the caption or the text.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>

    <AutoExample name="Colors" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="sizes">Sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Use <PropOrClass prop='size="small"' class=".ui-small" /> or <PropOrClass
        prop='size="large"'
        class=".ui-large"
      /> for a shorter or taller plot. Set <code>--_block-size</code> for any other
      height, and <code>--_bar-size</code> for the widest a bar gets.
    </p>

    <AutoExample name="Sizes" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="negative-values">Negative values</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="astro"
        >Negative values hang from the zero line. <code>min</code> defaults to the
        smallest value. <code>format</code> turns each value into the text of its
        cell, here with a sign.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >Negative values hang from the zero line. Set the smallest value in <code
          >--min</code
        >. The cell text is what people read, so write it the way it should
        show, here with a sign.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="svelte"
        >Negative values hang from the zero line. <code>min</code> defaults to the
        smallest value. <code>format</code> turns each value into the text of its
        cell, here with a sign.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="vue"
        >Negative values hang from the zero line. <code>min</code> defaults to the
        smallest value. <code>format</code> turns each value into the text of its
        cell, here with a sign.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>

    <AutoExample name="Negative" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="cell-text">Cell text</h2>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="astro"
        >Write the <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code> yourself
        in the default slot. Give every <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> its number in <code
          >--value</code
        >, and pass <code>max</code> (and <code>min</code> for negative values), since
        the component can't read the slot. The cell text can differ from the value.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >The bar draws <code>--value</code>, and people read the cell text, so
        the text can carry a unit.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="svelte"
        >Write the <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code> yourself
        as children. Give every <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> its number in <code
          >--value</code
        >, and pass <code>max</code> (and <code>min</code> for negative values), since
        the component can't read the children. The cell text can differ from the value.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="vue"
        >Write the <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code> yourself
        in the default slot. Give every <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> its number in <code
          >--value</code
        >, and pass <code>max</code> (and <code>min</code> for negative values), since
        the component can't read the slot. The cell text can differ from the value.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>

    <AutoExample name="Markup" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="limitations">Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <Conditional>
          <Fragment slot="astro"
            >The component writes every value to an inline <code>--value</code>,
            and <code>max</code> and <code>min</code> to <code>--max</code> and <code
              >--min</code
            >, so charts draw in all current browsers.</Fragment
          >
          <Fragment slot="html"
            >CSS can't find the largest value in a table, so <code>--max</code> and
            <code>--min</code> are yours to set. <code>data-value</code>, <code
              >data-max</code
            > and <code>data-min</code> work instead of the custom properties in Chromium
            only, through typed <code>attr()</code>. Firefox and Safari then
            draw flat bars over the readable table, so use the inline custom
            properties.</Fragment
          >
          <Fragment slot="svelte"
            >The component writes every value to an inline <code>--value</code>,
            and <code>max</code> and <code>min</code> to <code>--max</code> and <code
              >--min</code
            >, so charts draw in all current browsers.</Fragment
          >
          <Fragment slot="vue"
            >The component writes every value to an inline <code>--value</code>,
            and <code>max</code> and <code>min</code> to <code>--max</code> and <code
              >--min</code
            >, so charts draw in all current browsers.</Fragment
          >
        </Conditional>
      </li>
      <li>
        Axis labels are whole numbers, up to 10 of them, without units or locale
        formatting. Put the unit in the caption.
      </li>
      <li>
        Up to three series colors. Tooltips show the value only, and they don't
        flip at the edges.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each bar is an element, which is fine for dozens of bars, not thousands.
      </li>
      <li>Without <code>@starting-style</code>, the bars don't grow in.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="accessibility">
    <p>
      The chart is a <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>: screen readers read the
      caption, the series headers, a row header per category and every value.
      The axis labels are generated content with empty alternative text, so they
      aren't read.
    </p>
    <Conditional as="p">
      <Fragment slot="astro"
        >Every bar is a Tab stop that shows its value on focus. Set <code
          >focusable={"{false}"}</code
        > when the values are shown elsewhere.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="html"
        >With <code>tabindex="0"</code> on its <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>, every
        bar is a Tab stop that shows its value on focus. Leave it out when the
        values are shown elsewhere.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="svelte"
        >Every bar is a Tab stop that shows its value on focus. Set <code
          >focusable={"{false}"}</code
        > when the values are shown elsewhere.</Fragment
      >
      <Fragment slot="vue"
        >Every bar is a Tab stop that shows its value on focus. Set <code
          >:focusable="false"</code
        > when the values are shown elsewhere.</Fragment
      >
    </Conditional>
    <p>
      The three series colors are told apart with color vision deficiencies, and
      the legend names them. In forced colors mode the bars use <code
        >CanvasText</code
      >, with the second and third series hatched in opposite directions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bars grow in unless motion is off: with reduced motion, <code
        >.ui-motion-off</code
      > on the chart or a parent, or <code>--motion: 0</code> on a parent. In right-to-left
      pages the axis moves to the right and the categories run right to left.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</Component>
